# Copy to server/.env (gitignored). On Render, set these in the service's Environment tab.

# Operating timezone for check-in dates/times
APP_TIMEZONE=America/Chicago

# Keeps sessions valid across restarts/deploys (generate a long random string)
SESSION_SECRET=change-me

# Turso (libSQL) database for production persistence.
# Leave unset locally to use a local SQLite file (server/courier_logs.db).
# TURSO_DATABASE_URL=libsql://your-db.turso.io
# TURSO_AUTH_TOKEN=

# Bootstrap admin. Used only when the database has no admin user yet (first
# boot). After that, manage users through the users API; env changes do nothing.
ADMIN_USER=admin
ADMIN_PASS=change-me

# Two-factor authentication for staff (ticket 4.3). On in production by
# default: an admin, ops manager or dispatcher without a confirmed second
# factor can reach the enrolment endpoints and nothing else. Couriers are
# outside the policy; their second factor is the enrolled phone. Set this to
# true locally to rehearse the production behaviour.
# MFA_ENFORCED=true

# Reverse proxy hops in front of the app. Render puts exactly one there, which
# is the default in production. Zero (the default elsewhere) means trust
# nothing: with a non-zero value a client can set X-Forwarded-For and choose
# the address that lands in the audit trail.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# Pointing a local process at a real Turso database, deliberately: to inspect
# it, or to check a restore (docs/runbook.md). Without this the server refuses
# to start against Turso unless NODE_ENV=production, because a real database
# with NODE_ENV unset means insecure cookies, no HSTS, no MFA enforcement and
# no proxy trust, with a health check still saying ok.
# ALLOW_TURSO_OUTSIDE_PRODUCTION=true

# Address lookup (ticket 1.4). Absent means every lookup is refused with a
# message saying so, which is the state until a key exists.
#
# SITES ONLY. A pharmacy's street address is a business address. A patient's
# delivery address is PHI, Google Maps Platform is not covered by Google's BAA,
# and the code refuses to send one. See server/src/core/geo/provider.ts.
# GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=
# GEO_DAILY_CEILING=2500

# Draw a map of the stop INSIDE the courier app instead of linking out to
# Google Maps (ticket 5.13). Off, and it should stay off until somebody can
# point at an agreement that covers it.
#
# The link-out is a courier asking their own navigation app how to get
# somewhere. The embed is US sending a patient's address to Google, under our
# key, for every stop on every run, whether or not anybody taps anything.
# Google's BAA does not cover the Maps Platform. This is deliberately NOT
# implied by GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY above, which exists so pharmacy SITES can be
# geocoded, and that is lawful because a hospital's address is a business
# address. See server/src/modules/uh/directions.ts.
#
# Turning this on also opens frame-src on the CSP to www.google.com.
# UH_MAPS_EMBED=1

# How many days a courier's minute-by-minute track is kept (ticket 6.6).
#
# SETTING THIS IS THE DECISION, and while it is unset the tracking endpoint
# refuses every point. That is the opposite of how every other retention
# period in this system behaves, and it is deliberate: everywhere else the
# risk is deleting evidence too early, so undecided means keep. A breadcrumb
# trail of where an identified employee was all day has almost no operational
# value the day after the shift, and every day it is kept is a day it can be
# subpoenaed or breached. Collecting it with no agreed expiry is the harm.
#
# Days, not years. Nothing in the contract asks for this data at all, so the
# number cannot be inherited from the delivery record it sits beside.
# RETENTION_LOCATION_TRACE_DAYS=30

# How often the unclaimed sweep runs (tickets 6.5 and 6.8). Unset means it
# does not run at all, and the promise that an unclaimed STAT is never
# nobody's problem holds only while somebody remembers to call the endpoint.
#
# Running twice is harmless and there is a test for it: the sweep works from
# the unassigned pool, and claiming a stop goes through a UNIQUE index, so two
# instances sweeping together is wasted work rather than a double assignment.
# That is why this is an in-process timer and not a cron service with a lock.
# SWEEP_INTERVAL_SECONDS=120

# Runtime environment: development (default), test, or production.
# In production TURSO_DATABASE_URL is required and SESSION_SECRET must be 32+ chars.
# NODE_ENV=development

# Local SQLite file, relative to server/, used when TURSO_DATABASE_URL is unset.
# DB_FILE=courier_logs.db

# Proof-of-delivery file storage (ticket 1.8). When FILES_ENABLED=true all four
# S3 values are required. Use a bucket covered by a signed AWS BAA.
# FILES_ENABLED=false
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_REGION=
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_KMS_KEY_ID=

# Server-side session lifetimes (minutes). Idle expiry moves forward on use;
# absolute expiry does not. Staff = admin, ops manager, dispatcher, client
# viewer. Courier = drivers. Defaults: staff 30 idle / 720 absolute,
# courier 720 idle / 43200 absolute.
# SESSION_STAFF_IDLE_MINUTES=30
# SESSION_STAFF_ABSOLUTE_MINUTES=720
# SESSION_COURIER_IDLE_MINUTES=720
# SESSION_COURIER_ABSOLUTE_MINUTES=43200

# Logging. Level debug | info | warn | error (default info). Format json
# (default outside development) or pretty (default in development).
# LOG_LEVEL=info
# LOG_FORMAT=json
